WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:03.930
Now to recap, we are
checking if some number

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:08.280
N is prime by doing
a trial division.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.440
Here is the square root
of N. And here is 3.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.050
Starting at 3 and we are
hopping along by 2 up

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.880
until the square root of N.
And at each point in the way,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.280
checking if that number
divides N. So far,

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.050
people have been trying to
reduce the number of steps

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:30.670
we take, by perhaps, starting
later and taking larger steps.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.540
Now I just want to pause here.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.600
And let's think about what
is the ideal case for trial

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.700
division algorithm.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.740
What is the best
we could possibly

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.940
do if we got very
creative in our stepping?

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.830
Well remember, any number N
has some prime factorization.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.140
And let's say the square
root of N is here.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.510
We actually only need to
step on prime numbers.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.480
That would be the
best we could do.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.840
Because we know if we
step only on primes,

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.545
we will eventually
find a factor,

00:01:04.545 --> 00:01:07.700
a prime factor if
it's a composite.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.030
The question now is how
efficient is this method?

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.690
Because it seems like we
have a perfect solution now.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.700
If we wrote a new algorithm,
which first just called

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.480
the sieve.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.250
Let's say the new algorithm
calculating if N is prime.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.340
It could call the
sieve and generate

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.990
a nice long list
of primes for us.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.510
And then we would have
our trial division,

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.770
which would use
this list of primes.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.570
So it could hop along
and hit only primes,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.450
up until the square root
of N, wherever that is.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.920
So what's wrong with this?

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.970
Well, we can visualize
the time complexity,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.660
or the number of steps taken.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.030
And remember I did so by,
I coded up this algorithm.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.490
And I put in a step
counter inside each loop.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:04.850
So we have, let's just say, step
plus plus, means step plus 1

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:05.700
here.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.270
And inside this other loop,
there's also a step counter.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.990
Step plus plus.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.130
So these are all
constant operations

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.550
checking if and marking.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.460
So we just have a step
counter inside each loop.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.380
And now, here is a comparison.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.510
On the far left is our
old trial division method.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:29.130
In the middle is our algorithm,
just calling the sieve

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.420
to generate all primes
up to N. And on the right

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.840
is this proposal
where we just call

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.010
the sieve to generate primes
up to the square root of N.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:43.300
And then call trial division
just on those primes.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.780
And let's see what
happens with small input.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:53.020
As we can see initially,
the sieve takes many steps.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.920
So even the modified
version on the right

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.710
is actually slower
than trial division.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.980
And as the input grows, the
number of steps in the sieve

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.500
grows even faster.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.740
So let's just forget the middle
and compare trial division

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.640
versus the sieve up to
the square root of N

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.770
plus trial division.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.340
And here we can see
the old trial division

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.180
method is much more efficient.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.410
The number of steps in our sieve
to square root of N, plus trial

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.100
division is growing much faster.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.680
So it is actually
not an improvement.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.710
And below is the program I
used to do this comparison.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.250
And there is a recording
explaining how I set it up.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.180
But now you may be
wondering, well,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.840
what if we calculated
the primes in advance?

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.280
So the first step
would be to, let's say,

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:52.450
build an array of primes and
store it on a hard drive.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:57.210
And then our algorithm would
just do trial division.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.010
And it would know how
to hop on primes only,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:05.110
because it would be reading
from this proposed prime list.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.970
And perhaps our
prime list stores

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.190
all primes up to 20
digits, or even 100 digits.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.160
Why can't we do this?

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.430
Well, the problem is
limitations in memory.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.740
When we enumerate
lists of numbers,

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.230
which we'll explore next.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.290
And just for example, let's
say we were doing this by hand.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.370
We calculate 5 was prime.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.070
We write 5 on a piece
of paper and we store it

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.480
in a filing cabinet.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.780
Then we get 7.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.250
We store that in
a filing cabinet.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.950
9, or 11-- sorry-- into
the filing cabinet,

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.940
and then we have a filing
cabinet full of prime numbers.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:46.340
This is our, think of
it as a prime array.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.620
Now how big would this
filing cabinet be, if say,

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.160
we wanted all primes
up to 20 digits,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.080
or all primes up
to 100 digits long?

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.990
Could we even store this
array on a hard drive?

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.190
And to understand why this
actually isn't possible,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.940
we have to dive a little
deeper into how large does this

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.350
actually grow, this prime array?

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.960
And what is the size limitation
of modern-day and even future

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.040
computer hardware?

